<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Buco di Viso</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1480</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Monviso tunnel</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Buco di Viso is a tunnel about 75 metres long cut through the rock below the Colle delle Traversette, at nearly 2,900 metres near Monviso, on the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Marquis Ludovico II of Saluzzo had it dug around 1480 to carry salt, cloth and other goods by mule between Provence and Saluzzo. It is considered the oldest tunnel through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/buco-di-viso.jpg" alt="Buco di Viso" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ing.dox · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Buco_di_Viso.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
